linuwu-sense-dkms for Debian
============================

Replacing acer_wmi
------------------
This module replaces the kernel's acer_wmi: both bind to the same ACPI-WMI
GUIDs and cannot coexist. The package installs

    /usr/lib/modprobe.d/linuwu-sense.conf

with "blacklist acer_wmi" plus "install acer_wmi /bin/true" (the second is
needed because blacklisting alone does not stop the kernel autoloading it).
Removing the package removes the file and acer_wmi comes back.

The package does NOT unload modules during installation: doing so can hang the
machine if devices are in use. After installing, reboot, or run:

    sudo modprobe -r acer_wmi && sudo modprobe linuwu_sense

To go back to acer_wmi without uninstalling, create
/etc/modprobe.d/zz-local-acer-wmi.conf containing:

    install acer_wmi /sbin/modprobe --ignore-install acer_wmi

Secure Boot
-----------
With Secure Boot on, the module must be signed or the kernel refuses it ("Key
was rejected by service"). DKMS handles the signing, but the key has to be
enrolled once:

    sudo dkms generate_mok                       # if it does not exist yet
    sudo mokutil --import /var/lib/dkms/mok.pub
    # reboot, pick "Enroll MOK", type the password
    sudo dkms autoinstall

Check with "mokutil --sb-state". The key survives kernel upgrades: you enrol it
once and DKMS re-signs on every rebuild.

Permissions
-----------
The sysfs attributes belong to the linuwu_sense group. To use them without
sudo:

    sudo usermod -aG linuwu_sense $USER      # needs a new login session

Paths (depending on model):

    /sys/devices/platform/acer-wmi/predator_sense/
    /sys/devices/platform/acer-wmi/nitro_sense/
    /sys/devices/platform/acer-wmi/four_zoned_kb/

A udev rule reapplies those permissions on every module load. Without it they
are lost whenever the module is reloaded -- on a DKMS upgrade, for instance --
because the kernel recreates the attributes as root:root. The symptom is
confusing: the application asks you to join a group you are already in.

Four-zone RGB keyboard
----------------------
Per-zone colours need the zones to be ENABLED first, which upstream never did.
The sequence is:

    method 0x02  <-  BIT(3) | GENMASK_ULL(43,40)     enable the zones
    method 0x06  <-  zone | R<<8 | G<<16 | B<<24     one call per zone
    method 0x14  <-  KBLE=0, KBLS=0, brightness      switch on, static

Writing per_zone_mode does all three. Two things worth knowing:

 * KBLE = 0 is the STATIC effect, not "off". It looks like "off" because with
   no zones enabled there is nothing to paint.
 * Method 0x02 is absent from the WMBH block of this firmware's SSDT, which
   only implements 0x05-0x07 and 0x10-0x17. It answers anyway. Reading the
   ACPI to decide what is supported leads to the wrong conclusion here.

This was recovered by disassembling the Windows PredatorSense application.

Debug helper
------------
predator_sense/wmi_call invokes any gaming WMI method:

    echo "5,0" > .../predator_sense/wmi_call && cat .../wmi_call

Format is "<method>,<value>", value in decimal or 0x hex. It is a diagnostic
aid, not a stable interface, and calling methods blindly can leave the EC in a
strange state until the module is reloaded.

Differences from upstream
-------------------------
 * Compatibility with the pre-6.13 platform_profile API. Upstream uses
   devm_platform_profile_register() and platform_profile_notify(dev), which do
   not exist in Debian 13's 6.12 LTS kernel.
 * platform_profile is only registered if the firmware actually answers.
   Upstream registers unconditionally; Div-Linuwu-Sense queried a "supported
   profiles" method that many firmwares do not implement and gave up on
   failure. Neither is right: this fork falls back to the standard set when the
   firmware cannot enumerate but does respond, and registers nothing when it
   does not respond at all.
 * Predator PH315-53 quirk completed with predator_v4 and four_zone_kb.
 * Four-zone RGB fixed, as described above.
 * Makefile that honours KERNELRELEASE, needed by DKMS.

 -- Antonio Orizondo Leyva <aorizondoleyva@gmail.com>
